    "text": "Let E be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, equipped with its Borel measurable space and canonical Lebesgue measure dx; dimension zero is allowed. Let V:E->R and alpha,beta be nonnegative real scalars and eta a real scalar. Assume alpha>0, alpha<=beta, V is C2, and, for every x,v in E,\n  alpha*||v||^2 <= (fderiv R (fderiv R V) x v) v <= beta*||v||^2.\nAssume eta>0 and beta*eta<=1. Define\n  mu = volume.tilted(x -> -V(x)),\n  J = map((x,g) -> (x,x+sqrt(eta)*g), mu.prod(stdGaussian E)),\n  W_y(z) = V((y+z)/2) + ||z-y||^2/(8*eta).\nHere volume.tilted(-F) is the volume measure with density exp(-F)/integral exp(-F); the Gaussian/product/pushforward are the actual measures.\nThere exist kernels R,S:E->Measure E with all the following properties: R and S are Markov kernels; R is a conditional kernel for J.map Prod.swap, disintegrating the swapped joint (Y,X); and for every y in E, S(y) is the pushforward of R(y) under x -> 2*x-y.\nFor every y in E, S(y)=volume.tilted(z -> -W_y(z)), W_y is C2, exp(-W_y) is Lebesgue integrable, and its Lebesgue integral is strictly positive. Moreover, for this y there exists a real-linear partial operator\n  D:L2(S(y);R) -> L2(S(y);E)\nwhose domain is dense in L2(S(y);R), which is closable and whose closure is closed, and whose graph is characterized exactly as follows: for every a in L2(S(y);R) and H in L2(S(y);E),\n  (a,H) belongs to graph(D) iff there exists phi:E->R that is smooth and has compact support, with a=phi S(y)-almost everywhere and H=gradient(phi) S(y)-almost everywhere.\nFor this same D, for every real epsilon>0 and every f in L2(S(y);R), there exists u in domain(D.closure) satisfying all the following conclusions simultaneously. Write u_rep and f_rep for the usual representatives of the scalar L2 classes, and G_rep for the usual representative of G=D.closure(u) in L2(S(y);E).\n(1) For every v in domain(D.closure),\n  epsilon*<u,v>_{L2(S(y);R)} + <D.closure(u),D.closure(v)>_{L2(S(y);E)} = <f,v>_{L2(S(y);R)}.\n(2) u_rep and G_rep are locally integrable with respect to Lebesgue volume.\n(3) For every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R and every direction v in E, both x -> psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E and x -> u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) are Lebesgue integrable and\n  integral psi(x)*<G_rep(x),v>_E dx = - integral u_rep(x)*(fderiv R psi x v) dx.\n(4) For every C1 compactly supported psi:E->R, all three functions\n  x -> exp(-W_y(x))*(u_rep(x)*psi(x)),\n  x -> exp(-W_y(x))*<G_rep(x),gradient(psi)(x)>_E,\n  x -> exp(-W_y(x))*(f_rep(x)*psi(x))\nare Lebesgue integrable, and\n  epsilon*integral exp(-W_y(x))*(u_rep(x)*psi(x)) dx\n  + integral exp(-W_y(x))*<G_rep(x),gradient(psi)(x)>_E dx\n  = integral exp(-W_y(x))*(f_rep(x)*psi(x)) dx.\nOne pair of kernels R,S works for all y. For each y one D is chosen before epsilon and f, and works for all of them. For each epsilon and f the same u serves every variational test v, every directional weak-gradient test, and every weighted test psi. The Hilbert inner products use S(y), whereas all local-integrability assertions, directional weak-gradient integrals, partition integrals, and the final weighted test integrals use Lebesgue volume. The displayed weighted equation uses exp(-W_y) without dividing by its partition integral. The conclusions about S(y), W_y, and D hold for every y, not merely almost every y; the conditional-kernel conclusion itself has its usual disintegration meaning.",
